          <span class="metric-value">Sr/Ca in Sclerosponges</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOP TWO-COLUMN: Why Sclerosponges + Pre-Industrial Baseline -->
    <div class="two-col">
      <section class="col animate delay-1" aria-label="Why sclerosponges">
        <h2 class="col-title">Why Sclerosponges?</h2>
        <div class="col-body">
          <p>Sclerosponges are <strong>long-lived marine organisms</strong> found in tropical and subtropical oceans. As they grow, strontium-to-calcium (Sr/Ca) ratios in their aragonite skeletons faithfully track ambient water temperature.</p>
          <p><strong>Continuous growth</strong> over centuries yields unbroken annual-resolution records — extending well before instrumental coverage begins around 1850.</p>
          <p>Specimens from Caribbean reefs (33–91 m depth) capture large-scale SST variability. Calibration against co-located instrumental records from 1960 onward achieves <strong>r&sup2; &gt; 0.9</strong>.</p>
          <p>Validated against HadCRUT5 and NOAA ERSSTv5 — excellent agreement during the instrumental era, with divergence only before ~1850, precisely where the hidden warming resides.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="col animate delay-2" aria-label="Pre-industrial baseline comparison">
        <h2 class="col-title">Pre-Industrial Baseline Matters</h2>
        <div class="col-body">
          <p>The IPCC's 1850–1900 "pre-industrial" reference period <strong>already contained ~0.5 °C of anthropogenic warming</strong>. When measured against the true pre-industrial baseline (1700–1790), total warming is substantially higher:</p>

          <div class="temp-compare">
            <div class="temp-bar-group">
              <div class="temp-bar ipcc">~1.2 °C</div>
              <div class="temp-bar-label">IPCC Standard<br>1850–1900 baseline</div>
            </div>
            <div class="temp-bar-group">
              <div class="temp-bar true">~1.7 °C</div>
              <div class="temp-bar-label">True Pre-Industrial<br>1700–1790 baseline</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="hidden-callout">
            <div class="big-num">+0.5 °C</div>
            <div class="label">Hidden warming already occurred before the conventional "pre-industrial" baseline even begins</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- CENTERPIECE: 300-Year Temperature Reconstruction -->
    <section class="chart-section animate delay-3" aria-label="300-year temperature reconstruction chart" role="img">
      <h2 class="chart-section-title">300-Year Temperature Reconstruction</h2>
      <p class="chart-section-subtitle">Sclerosponge Sr/Ca proxy record + instrumental data. Anomaly relative to 1700–1790 mean.</p>
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="tempChart" role="img" aria-label="Line chart showing 300-year temperature anomaly reconstruction from sclerosponge proxies. Temperature anomaly rises from near zero in 1700 to approximately 1.7 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial baseline by 2020, crossing the 1.5 degree Paris Agreement threshold around 2010. A dashed red line marks the 1.5 degree threshold."></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-only">Sclerosponge temperature reconstruction shows warming from ~0°C in 1700 to ~1.7°C in 2020. Crosses 1.5°C threshold around 2010. The 1850–1900 IPCC baseline period was already ~0.5°C above true pre-industrial levels.</div>

      <div class="milestones">
        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1700–1790</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Stable pre-industrial baseline</span></div>
        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1790–1850</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Early industrial warming onset</span></div>
        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1850–1900</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">~0.5 °C above baseline</span></div>
        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1900–1960</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Gradual warming continues</span></div>
        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1960–2000</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Accelerating trend</span></div>
        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">2000–2020</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">&gt;1.5 °C exceeded</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BOTTOM TWO-COLUMN: Policy + Methods -->
    <div class="two-col">
      <section class="col animate delay-4" aria-label="Policy and climate target implications">
        <h2 class="col-title">Policy &amp; Climate Target Implications</h2>
        <div class="impl-list">
          <div class="impl-item">
            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
            <span>The Paris Agreement's 1.5 °C target is framed relative to "pre-industrial levels," but the IPCC operationally uses <strong>1850–1900</strong> — a period that was <strong>not truly pre-industrial</strong>.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="impl-item">
            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
            <span>Adopting the sclerosponge-informed baseline reduces the <strong>remaining carbon budget for 1.5 °C to effectively zero</strong>.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="impl-item">
            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
            <span>The 2.0 °C guardrail is closer than estimated: only <strong>~0.3 °C of additional warming</strong> remains, not ~0.8 °C as previously reported.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="impl-item">
            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
            <span>These findings underscore the <strong>urgency of rapid emissions reductions</strong> and transparent communication about how baseline choices affect perceived climate targets.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="col animate delay-5" aria-label="Data and methods">
        <h2 class="col-title">Data &amp; Methods</h2>
        <div class="method-item">
          <div class="method-icon">1</div>
          <div class="method-text"><strong>Sample Collection:</strong> Sclerosponge specimens collected from Caribbean reef environments at depths of 33–91 m.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="method-item">
          <div class="method-icon">2</div>
          <div class="method-text"><strong>Geochemical Analysis:</strong> Sr/Ca ratios measured via ICP-OES and calibrated against co-located instrumental SST records (r&sup2; &gt; 0.9).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="method-item">
          <div class="method-icon">3</div>
          <div class="method-text"><strong>Validation:</strong> Reconstruction validated against HadCRUT5 and NOAA ERSSTv5 over overlapping intervals — excellent agreement during instrumental era.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="method-item">
          <div class="method-icon">4</div>
          <div class="method-text"><strong>Key Finding:</strong> Significant divergence from instrumental records before ~1850 reveals the hidden pre-industrial warming component (~0.5 °C).</div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- FOOTER -->
    <section class="poster-footer animate delay-5" aria-label="Reference and attribution">
      <span class="footer-ref">Reference: Nature Climate Change (2024). Sclerosponge-based temperature proxy reconstruction, Caribbean Sea.</span>
      <span class="footer-ref">Presented at AGU / Nature Climate Change Symposium</span>
    </section>

  </main>

