results.content:
460	    <!-- CENTERPIECE: 300-Year Temperature Reconstruction -->
461	    <section class="chart-section animate delay-3" aria-label="300-year temperature reconstruction chart" role="img">
462	      <h2 class="chart-section-title">300-Year Temperature Reconstruction</h2>
463	      <p class="chart-section-subtitle">Sclerosponge Sr/Ca proxy record + instrumental data. Anomaly relative to 1700–1790 mean.</p>
464	      <div class="chart-wrap">
465	        <canvas id="tempChart" role="img" aria-label="Line chart showing 300-year temperature anomaly reconstruction from sclerosponge proxies. Temperature anomaly rises from near zero in 1700 to approximately 1.7 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial baseline by 2020, crossing the 1.5 degree Paris Agreement threshold around 2010. A dashed red line marks the 1.5 degree threshold."></canvas>
466	      </div>
467	      <div class="sr-only">Sclerosponge temperature reconstruction shows warming from ~0°C in 1700 to ~1.7°C in 2020. Crosses 1.5°C threshold around 2010. The 1850–1900 IPCC baseline period was already ~0.5°C above true pre-industrial levels.</div>
468	
469	      <div class="milestones">
470	        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1700–1790</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Stable pre-industrial baseline</span></div>
471	        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1790–1850</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Early industrial warming onset</span></div>
472	        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1850–1900</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">~0.5 °C above baseline</span></div>
473	        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1900–1960</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Gradual warming continues</span></div>
474	        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">1960–2000</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">Accelerating trend</span></div>
475	        <div class="milestone"><span class="milestone-period">2000–2020</span><br><span class="milestone-desc">&gt;1.5 °C exceeded</span></div>
476	      </div>
477	    </section>
478	
479	    <!-- BOTTOM TWO-COLUMN: Policy + Methods -->
480	    <div class="two-col">
481	      <section class="col animate delay-4" aria-label="Policy and climate target implications">
482	        <h2 class="col-title">Policy &amp; Climate Target Implications</h2>
483	        <div class="impl-list">
484	          <div class="impl-item">
485	            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
486	            <span>The Paris Agreement's 1.5 °C target is framed relative to "pre-industrial levels," but the IPCC operationally uses <strong>1850–1900</strong> — a period that was <strong>not truly pre-industrial</strong>.</span>
487	          </div>
488	          <div class="impl-item">
489	            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
490	            <span>Adopting the sclerosponge-informed baseline reduces the <strong>remaining carbon budget for 1.5 °C to effectively zero</strong>.</span>
491	          </div>
492	          <div class="impl-item">
493	            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
494	            <span>The 2.0 °C guardrail is closer than estimated: only <strong>~0.3 °C of additional warming</strong> remains, not ~0.8 °C as previously reported.</span>
495	          </div>
496	          <div class="impl-item">
497	            <div class="impl-marker"></div>
498	            <span>These findings underscore the <strong>urgency of rapid emissions reductions</strong> and transparent communication about how baseline choices affect perceived climate targets.</span>
499	          </div>
500	        </div>
501	      </section>
502	
503	      <section class="col animate delay-5" aria-label="Data and methods">
504	        <h2 class="col-title">Data &amp; Methods</h2>
505	        <div class="method-item">
506	          <div class="method-icon">1</div>
507	          <div class="method-text"><strong>Sample Collection:</strong> Sclerosponge specimens collected from Caribbean reef environments at depths of 33–91 m.</div>
508	        </div>
509	        <div class="method-item">
510	          <div class="method-icon">2</div>
511	          <div class="method-text"><strong>Geochemical Analysis:</strong> Sr/Ca ratios measured via ICP-OES and calibrated against co-located instrumental SST records (r&sup2; &gt; 0.9).</div>
512	        </div>
513	        <div class="method-item">
514	          <div class="method-icon">3</div>
515	          <div class="method-text"><strong>Validation:</strong> Reconstruction validated against HadCRUT5 and NOAA ERSSTv5 over overlapping intervals — excellent agreement during instrumental era.</div>
516	        </div>
517	        <div class="method-item">
518	          <div class="method-icon">4</div>
519	          <div class="method-text"><strong>Key Finding:</strong> Significant divergence from instrumental records before ~1850 reveals the hidden pre-industrial warming component (~0.5 °C).</div>
520	        </div>
521	      </section>
522	    </div>
523	
524	    <!-- FOOTER -->
525	    <section class="poster-footer animate delay-5" aria-label="Reference and attribution">
526	      <span class="footer-ref">Reference: Nature Climate Change (2024). Sclerosponge-based temperature proxy reconstruction, Caribbean Sea.</span>
527	      <span class="footer-ref">Presented at AGU / Nature Climate Change Symposium</span>
528	    </section>
